From 53fa0be9d7e0af63defe5f470bc4b4d542fae992 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: abhay-byte <zenithblue.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 02:39:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] wsi/x11: collect sw MIT-SHM present replies on the event
 thread (PANVK_WSI_SW_DEFER_REPLY)

csf-v11/117 made the sw present copy each frame into a memfd segment,
send ShmPutImage and then a GetGeometry, and block the present thread
(with thread_state_lock held) on the GetGeometry reply: the reply both
proves that the server has read the segment and reports a resize.
That puts the X server's whole handling of the put, plus a socket round
trip, on the critical path of every present.  PanPlay's built-in X
server is Java, so the put is not cheap, and the present thread can
neither copy the next frame nor hand the image back to the application
meanwhile.  The D3D11 cube sits at 47 fps with the GPU about 49 % busy
and 0.6 CPU cores used: a serialized chain, not a busy one.

With wsi_device::x11.sw_defer_reply set (sw_shm chains only):

- The present thread copies, sends the put and the GetGeometry,
  flushes, records the cookie in the image (sequence, submission
  serial, present ID) and gives the image back to the acquire queue at
  once: the server only reads the segment, never the image.
- The event thread, idle on the sw path so far, collects the oldest
  outstanding reply, reports OUT_OF_DATE or SURFACE_LOST through
  x11_swapchain_result(), completes the present ID and wakes the
  present thread.
- Before copying into an image's segment, the present thread waits
  until that image's previous reply is in, which keeps 117's guarantee
  that the copy cannot race with the server.  It also keeps at most two
  puts in flight, so the server always has the next frame queued while
  the window lags at most one frame behind.
- Destroy discards the replies nobody collected, after both threads
  have exited.

OUT_OF_DATE on a resize is now reported one present later.  The
synchronous path is unchanged apart from sharing the geometry and
present-ID helpers.

PanVK sets the option in panvk_wsi_init() from PANVK_WSI_SW_DEFER_REPLY
(default 1), a panvk_perf_knobs registry bit resolved once at the first
vkCreateInstance.

Rationale: perf review 2026-10-26 C2 (b), "X11 software present: CPU
read from write-combined memory and a blocking round trip per
present".  Knob: PANVK_WSI_SW_DEFER_REPLY=0 restores the 114
behaviour (blocking reply in the present thread).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
